27}{\rtlch \ltrch\super\loch
th}{\loch
 Feb 2026.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
The PicoRX.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
A few years ago I submitted an entry which used a Raspberry Pi Pico\u8217\'92s PIO subsystem as a 0-30MHz quadrature signal generator. That idea has been taken to a considerably higher level by Jon Dawson and his design }{\loch
as }{\loch
published online in GitHub and 101 Things. I am sure that he did not copy my signal generator design because it seems to be an obvious thing to do with it.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
The SEMARC club has chosen this project for 2026 and will design PCBs and housings.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
His PicoRX is a fully-featured minimal-cost long-wave to 30MHz receiver and decodes AM, FM, LSB, USB and CW. All it requires is a \u163\'a35 Pico, cheap OLED display, 3 push-buttons, shaft encoder, cheap dual op-amp and a multiplexer IC, plus a few resistors and capacitors. The receiver can be improved by the addition of band-pass filters, }{\loch
a small TFT screen}{\loch
 and an Si5351 module if one really wants to splash out. His design uses surface-mount low-pass filters but my build will use larger components with legs, for experimentation.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
The Pico has two 12-bit ADCs and they collect I and Q samples from a low IF of DC to 12kHz, }{\loch
from a Tayloe mixer.}{\loch
 Oversampling is enabled and that gives an improvement on the dynamic range. A pre-amp would be useful for the higher bands but on 40m the performance is perfectly acceptable though }{\loch
Jon states that }{\loch
broadcast interference could be an issue at night. }{\loch
I have yet to try filters but they would be useful in attenuating }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
any}{\loch
 reception at multiples of the operating frequency.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
There is excellent AGC,  }{\loch
it\u8217\'92s }{\loch
all done in software }{\loch
as opposed to PIN diodes etc.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
Another brilliant feature is the USB interface }{\loch
(USB as in Universal Serial Bus) }{\loch
which provides }{\loch
both }{\loch
audio output and CAT commands. This is similar to rigs costing }{\loch
upwards of }{\loch
\u163\'a31,000. FLDIGI can be used to decode RTTY, PSK and MFSK signals. I have also used }{\loch
the USB CAT}{\loch
 with EI5DI\u8217\'92s SD logger }{\loch
so that typing a frequency into the call-sign box causes the PicoRX to jump to that frequency}{\loch
. }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
CAT }{\loch
also runs under DXLab Commander, so a PC can be used to select mode and frequency. CAT control is sent }{\loch
via}{\loch
 COM3 on this Windows PC. }{\loch
However I have not yet managed to get FLRIG to play.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
Another problem is that there are audible clicks when the tuning knob is rotated and Jon suggests that it is because the CPU clock is being reset to another frequency. That is done because the PIO subsystem cannot generate }{\loch
enough frequency resolution on the higher bands.}{\loch
 }
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
There are many Amateurs who are not keen on rigs with loads of menus and far prefer buttons, switches and knobs to perform the desired function. I have modified the software such that it uses two unallocated UART pins on the Pico to enable CAT by serial data. For a proof-of-concept I have connected this new serial data pair to a PIC which reads three push-buttons and controls green and red LEDs in each of the three switches. I have labelled these switches USB, CW and LSB. When a switch is pressed, its green LED is switched on and the relevant CAT command sent to the Pico at 19,200Bd. Poll commands are sent every second and the return packet is used to clear the green LED and switch on the correct red LED. In principal, I can extend the push-button scheme by adding more PICs, and I could also add potentiometers easily and }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
build}{\loch
 a menu-free }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
receiver}{\loch
.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
Because the minimum build would cost about \u163\'a310 + PCB, it is ideal for schools. There are 512 memories covering not only }{\loch
most }{\loch
Amateur bands but also LW, MW and SW broadcast stations, plus all the 27MHz CB channels. These may be easily changed and downloaded into the Pico }{\loch
using a CSV formatted file.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24\alang1081\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\loch\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\ql{\loch
I intend to generate an Amateur-only CSV file }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
which}{\loch
 include}{\loch
s}{\loch
 }{\rtlch\dbch\af5\langfe1081\dbch\af6\afs24 \ltrch\cf0\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\loch
all our band allocations. These can include the most popular FreeDV}{\loch
 channels as that is a very exciting \u8216\'91new\u8217\'92 mode (I think it\u8217\'92s over 10 years old actually!); FreeDV can be decoded by a free program running on a PC or laptop and connected with the USB CAT/audio cable. }
